'''Marsha Francine Warfield''' (born March 5, 1954) is an American actress and comedian. She grew up on Chicago's [[South Side, Chicago|South Side]],<ref>{{cite web| last1=Jesse and Jill| title=Marsha Warfield talks early days of comedy, coming out and her South Side ties (Audio)| url=https://soundcloud.com/vocalo/marsha-warfield| website=[[SoundCloud]]| access-date=February 25, 2018| date=February 9, 2018}}{{dead link|date=February 2026}}</ref> graduating from [[Calumet High School (Chicago)|Calumet High School]]. She is best known for playing tough, no-nonsense [[bailiff]] Roz Russell on the [[NBC]] sitcom ''[[Night Court]]'' from 1986 to 1992, reprising the role on a guest basis for its [[Night Court (2023 TV series)|2023 revival]].<ref>{{cite news| last=Kaufman| first=Joanne| title=Marsha Warfield Won't Be Fenced in by the Jinx of TV's Night Court| url=http://people.com/archive/marsha-warfield-wont-be-fenced-in-by-the-jinx-of-tvs-night-court-vol-29-no-6/| magazine=[[People (magazine)|People]]| access-date=February 25, 2018| date=February 15, 1988}}</ref><ref name="Scott">{{cite news| url=http://www.fredericksburg.com/entertainment/back-in-the-game-all-rise-for-marsha-warfield/article_c756a069-687c-5834-9a67-d9527fffed64.html| title=Back in the game: All rise for Marsha Warfield| first=Jesse| last=Scott| newspaper=[[The Free Lance-Star]]| location=Fredericksburg, Virginia|date=September 21, 2016| url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=2023-05-10 |title=Night Court Video: Marsha Warfield Returns as Roz in the Season Finale — But Is She Joining the Cast Full-Time? |url=https://www.tvline.com/casting-news/night-court-marsha-warfield-returns-roz-season-1-finale-recap-1234983578/ |access-date=2023-05-14 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Cordero |first=Rosy |date=2023-05-10 |title='Night Court' Finale Welcomes Back Original Series Co-Star Marsha Warfield As Roz |url=https://deadline.com/2023/05/night-court-finale-marsha-warfield-roz-1235361515/ |access-date=2023-05-14 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> Warfield also starred in the sitcom ''[[Empty Nest]]'' as Dr. Maxine Douglas (1993–1995). Before ''Night Court'', she was a writer and performer on the short-lived ''[[The Richard Pryor Show|Richard Pryor Show]]''.<ref name="Scott"/>

Warfield appeared in feature films such as ''[[D.C. Cab]]'' (1983) and ''[[Mask (1985 film)|Mask]]'' (1985), hosted ''[[The Marsha Warfield Show]]'' for ten months (March 1990–January 1991) and has made guest appearances on many television shows, including ''[[Riptide (American TV series)|Riptide]]'', ''[[Family Ties]]'', ''[[Clueless (TV series)|Clueless]]'', ''[[Cheers]]'', ''[[Living Single]]'', ''[[In Living Color]]'', ''[[Cybill]]'', ''[[Moesha]],'' [[Smart Guy]], and ''[[Touched by an Angel]]''. Her early stand-up comedy career is profiled alongside those of [[Rhonda Hansome]], Alice Arthur, and Jane Galvin Lewis in the 1984 documentary ''I Be Done Been Was Is'' by director Debra J. Robinson. As of 2024 she has a recurring role on [[List of 9-1-1 characters|9-1-1]] as the mother of main character paramedic and firefighter Henrietta "Hen" Wilson, played by [[Aisha Hinds]].

She has also done [[stand-up comedy]] including appearances on the ''[[Norm Crosby]]'' hosted ''The Comedy Shop'' television series.<ref>{{cite web| last1=AIRED: 9/29/78|title=The Comedy Shop Season 1 Episode 4 Featuring: Marsha Warfield|url=http://www.tv.com/shows/the-comedy-shop/episode-4-2387439/| website=[[TV.com]]| access-date=25 February 2018| archive-date=26 February 2018| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180226032831/http://www.tv.com/shows/the-comedy-shop/episode-4-2387439/|url-status=usurped| df=mdy-all}}</ref> She won the San Francisco International Comedy Competition in 1979, over such competition as [[Dana Carvey]] and [[A. Whitney Brown]].

==Personal life==
In 2017, Warfield publicly [[Coming out|came out]] stating:

{{blockquote|When I told my mother I was gay, she said she knew, and had known all my life.
Then, she asked me not to come out publicly while she was alive.
I agreed, even though the request and her admission were hurtful in ways I couldn't put my finger on then, and probably haven't completely worked through it now, but, everybody who knew me, knew I was gay.
The people I didn't tell knew anyway, and tacitly agreed to pretend that the unacknowledged had been acknowledged and accepted.
Like I'm sure is true for millions of other glass door closeted people.
When I went to bars, which was frequently, I never tried to hide who I was, so, it was an open secret.
Had I never come out publicly, many, many people would have known.
It would not then have ever really been a betrayal of trust to "spill the beans" because it wasn't a secret, it was an uncomfortably kept promise to my mother, but, it was also not the only reason I didn't come out swinging when she passed.
The fear of the judgment of strangers and their holier-than-thou "shoulds" was at least as big of a burden to bear, but the "shoulds" that "should" matter, don't.
Nobody should have to hide their sexuality.
No parent should ask their child to.
There should be no shame in being gay.<ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.facebook.com/MarshaWarfield/posts/1680744535297337| title=Log In or Sign Up to View| website=[[Facebook]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| last=Wells| first=Veronica| title=Comedian Marsha Warfield: My Mom Asked Me To Wait Until She Was Dead To Come Out, Not Mad At Patti LaBelle For "Outing" Luther| url=http://madamenoire.com/1008661/comedian-marsha-warfield-my-mother-asked-me-to-wait-until-she-was-dead-to-come-out-says-shes-not-mad-at-patti-labelle-for-outing-luther-vandross/| website=[[MadameNoire]]| access-date=25 February 2018| date=11 December 2017| df=mdy-all}}</ref>}}

Warfield's ''Night Court'' character, Roz, was portrayed as gay and marrying another woman  in the 2023 revival, something Warfield says could not have happened at the time of the original show.<ref>{{cite news |title=Marsha Warfield: Roz couldn't have been gay on Night Court |url=https://www.advocate.com/television/marsha-warfield-roz-gay |first=Trudy |last=Ring |magazine=[[The Advocate (magazine)|The Advocate]] |date=January 6, 2024| access-date=February 23, 2026 |language=en |quote=Marsha Warfield, whose Night Court character, Roz, has been revealed as gay in the reboot of the series, says that portrayal wouldn't have been possible during the show's original run.}}</ref>

Warfield is married to Angie Maldonado.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nm8U5Xkmn1g |title=A Heckling Romance - Marsha Warfield and Angie Maldonado |date=2024-10-03 |last=Pod XPerience Podcast Studio |access-date=2026-05-26 |via=YouTube}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Night Court's Marsha Warfield on living out and proud {{!}} Advocate.com |url=https://www.advocate.com/arts-entertainment/night-court-marsha-warfield-interview |access-date=2026-05-26 |website=www.advocate.com |language=en}}</ref>

